PR3 of the four-PR closure sequence for INV-fibis / INV-nuhun.

rust.py carries a 77-name denylist so a name-only resolution cannot bind
`x.load()` to a project `JoltDevice::load` -- WI-bakak measured 22 of 29 false
callers -- and that policy is CORRECT and untouched. But NINE of those names
are methods rust.yaml itself declares as I/O sinks (send, write, read, flush,
recv, new, spawn, status, output; ten rows, `write` twice), so
`sock.send(payload)` emitted NO EDGE AT ALL and a clean net_send verdict had
nothing to disclose. Measured: a crate whose exfiltrate() does exactly that
returned a BARE confirmed.

THE DENYLIST WAS DOING TWO JOBS AND ONLY ONE WAS EVER JUSTIFIED. It skips
RESOLUTION (WI-bakak's fix) and it also skipped emitting the honest
unresolved-external edge -- which points at `external` and could never have
produced one of those false callers.

THE SET MOVED RATHER THAN BEING COPIED. It lives in analyzer_disclosure and
rust.py imports it, so there is ONE object; a test asserts identity, not
equality. The two readers want OPPOSITE things from it -- the analyzer asks
"may I resolve this name?", verify_claims asks "what did I therefore never
look at?" -- which is exactly when two homes diverge unnoticed. The overlap
with the catalogue is computed at render time, so adding a row for a
denylisted name extends the disclosure with nobody remembering to.

EMITTING THE SUPPRESSED CALLS WAS MEASURED AND REJECTED, not assumed away: as
ordinary unresolved-external edges they take TOTAL edges +33% (bellman
2867->3819) and +67% (tiktoken-rs 375->627), and the EXTERNAL population +115%
and +207%, because the same denylist holds clone/unwrap/map.

A FIRST READING OF THE OVERLAP CLAIMED ALL FOUR Command ROWS WERE UNREACHABLE
AND A LIVE CONTROL REFUTED IT: `Command::new("sh").status()` returns violated
with the launch site named, because the denylist governs the INSTANCE-METHOD
path and `Command::new` is an associated-function call that catches the chain
first. The helper is method-kind-only for that reason.

Measured end to end with two controls:
  rust, denylisted sink name  -> confirmed_with_caveats rc 3, names all nine
  go                          -> BARE confirmed rc 0   (not a blanket downgrade)
  rust, non-denylisted sink   -> BARE confirmed        (not just reprinting
                                 the denylist)
  taint arm                   -> carries the same caveat (INV-nuhun asymmetry)

The remaining imprecision is stated rather than hidden: the caveat is
language-level, so a Rust crate calling none of the nine still receives it.
Scoping it needs an analyzer->verdict channel and therefore schema surface --
filed as WI-kodig rather than bolted on here.

